@@ -2,7 +2,7 @@
 
 Harness is a local-first control plane for coding agents. It preserves project intelligence across agent sessions and hosts while keeping source editing, shell access, Git, and browsing in the native host.
 
-> **Repository status:** implementation foundation. Packaging/tooling, SQLite persistence, Project/Workspace registry primitives, read-only runtime doctor checks, Workspace status, deterministic scan, and bounded indexed-path search CLIs, bounded daemon/local-IPC status/search/scan paths, POSIX daemon singleton/stale-socket recovery, canonical POSIX lazy daemon autostart, deterministic file-index reconciliation, bounded indexed-path search, durable internal Task/CAS, mechanical Task baselines, internal changed-file calculation, durable internal checkpoint/event persistence, and canonical POSIX per-user runtime paths exist; public Task start/resume/checkpoint, MCP, watcher, host integration, richer search channels, Knowledge, and dashboard behavior are not implemented yet.
+> **Repository status:** implementation foundation. Packaging/tooling, SQLite persistence, Project/Workspace registry primitives, read-only runtime doctor checks, Workspace status, deterministic scan, bounded indexed-path search, POSIX daemon lifecycle/autostart, durable Task/CAS with mechanical baselines/checkpoints, provenance-bearing Knowledge staleness, daemon Task IPC, and the official MCP v2 stdio bridge exist. Watcher behavior, host registration/adapters, richer search/context channels, skills, dashboard behavior, and final install/uninstall orchestration are not implemented yet.
 
 ## Product principles
 
@@ -50,13 +50,13 @@ The baseline-aware internal creation path now atomically captures the required m
 
 The internal changed-file calculator now compares that durable baseline with a stable current Git/worktree sample without mutating Task state or consulting the Structural Index. Exact unchanged non-opaque pre-existing dirty records are excluded; new dirtiness, changed/cleaned pre-existing dirtiness, conservative opaque entries, and committed tree deltas since the baseline HEAD are included. Task-time rename/copy records contribute both destination and origin paths. The calculation shares a finite deadline across baseline-row streaming, Git identity, dirty-content fingerprinting, committed-tree comparison, and deterministic result merging, and it fails closed if Workspace/Git state changes during the read.
 
-The internal checkpoint persistence layer now applies a checkpoint only to the explicitly named `working` Task using `task_id` plus `expected_revision`. A successful checkpoint increments Task revision exactly once and atomically stores bounded summary/next-step semantics, checkpoint state/wait reason, cumulative mechanical changed paths since Task start, current Git HEAD/branch/dirty-path count, and an immutable checkpoint timeline event. Stale writers and mechanical calculation failures are fully non-mutating. This is still not the public `task_start`/`task_checkpoint` workflow: resume orchestration, verification, Knowledge, bridge/session activity, daemon IPC, CLI, and MCP exposure remain later bounded slices.
+The checkpoint persistence layer applies a checkpoint only to the explicitly named `working` Task using `task_id` plus `expected_revision`. A successful checkpoint increments Task revision exactly once and atomically stores bounded summary/next-step semantics, checkpoint state/wait reason, cumulative mechanical changed paths since Task start, current Git HEAD/branch/dirty-path count, provenance-bearing Knowledge, and an immutable checkpoint timeline event. Stale writers and mechanical calculation failures are fully non-mutating. Public `task_start`/resume and `task_checkpoint` are exposed through bounded daemon IPC and the MCP bridge; verification records, dashboard mutations, and bridge activity history remain later slices.
 
 New Projects are created with the required `normal` visibility default. The low-level explicit registration primitive remains internal and requires an explicit Project identity plus an inspectable Git worktree; Harness stores the canonical worktree root and Git common directory reported by Git, treats repeat registration as idempotent, fails closed on incompatible root/Project identity, and rejects contradictory visibility policy for Workspaces sharing one Git common directory. Public scan registration reuses an already registered root, attaches a new linked worktree to the one unambiguous existing Project sharing its Git common directory, and creates a new Project for a Git checkout whose common directory is not registered. It does not infer logical Project identity across independent clones or expose visibility-mode transitions.
 
 The first deterministic index slice inventories regular files and symlinks for one registered Workspace, stores only relative path, entry kind, byte size, and SHA-256 mechanical identity, and reconciles additions/changes/deletions transactionally. Candidate enumeration uses Git tracked/untracked state with standard Git ignores, `.harnessignore`, common generated-directory exclusions, and default sensitive patterns such as `.env`, PEM, and key files. Symlink targets are hashed as link metadata rather than followed for source content, and symlinked parent paths that escape the Workspace fail closed. The index is derived/rebuildable data. Daemon-backed scans carry a finite execution deadline; deadline expiry rolls back an in-progress index reconciliation. This slice still does not run a filesystem watcher, parse symbols/languages, or populate FTS.
 
-The first search slice performs deterministic, Workspace-scoped retrieval over current `indexed_files` rows without reading or returning source text. It ranks exact relative-path matches, exact filenames, normalized ASCII identifier-token matches (including common camel/snake/kebab/dotted forms), then case-insensitive path substrings. Queries are bounded to 256 UTF-8 bytes, result count is hard-bounded to 50 with a default of 10, and each hit exposes only relative path, entry kind, size, and the mechanical match reason. The installed CLI exposes this as `harness search QUERY [PATH] [--limit N] [--socket PATH]`: `PATH` defaults to the current directory and must resolve inside an already registered Workspace. Search is read-only and uses the current persisted Structural Index snapshot; until the watcher exists, run `harness scan` to reconcile filesystem changes before relying on fresh results. With no `--socket`, search validates/reuses the canonical per-user daemon and lazily starts it when needed; an explicit socket remains caller-managed. Search result paths are JSON-quoted before terminal output so control characters in repository filenames cannot inject extra lines. The internal IPC response remains capped at 16 KiB; an oversized requested result set fails with a bounded `response_too_large` error instead of emitting a partial payload. Invalid/corrupted persisted index rows fail as bounded `index_error` responses without terminating the daemon. MCP `project_search`, FTS/content search, symbols, docs, Git retrieval, Knowledge, Working Sets, and rank fusion remain separate later slices.
